Z||000000||AuNROD||12-11-2001||10:12 AM||||was looking at an 88GT, v6, the other day and notice the temp or coolant gauge has gone completely off the gauge range. it is holding at the 1 o'clock possession. does anyone know why if this could be a bad sensor, gauge or faulty wiring? not to good to drive without a temp gauge. thanks.||||reg||11
Z||000001||AuNROD||12-11-2001||11:57 AM||||sorry about this bump, but come on all you fiero nuts. i need a little help. i'm thinking on buying this 88, but don't want to buy a lots of problems. so, what do you think the problem is with this gauge?||||reg||9
Z||000002||Voytek||12-11-2001||01:17 PM||||My '87 GT sticks to the upper left sometimes when I start the car.  It goes to normal position when I flick it and then works fine.<P>Could be two things:<BR>1.  The gauge itself sticks - a little bit of hassle (gauge disassembly) and some lubricant should get it working properly.<P>2.  The sending unit could be toast.  They're cheap (about $40 bucks here in Canada, I believe).  Very easy to replace too.  Just to the left on the engine block(looking from the back) below the ignition coil.  It's a brass fitting that has a wire or two (I don't remember) sticking out of it.<P>I wouldn't pass up the car just because of this.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||AuNROD||12-11-2001||07:27 PM||||thanks,Voytek, for the help. for a while i was wondering if this machine, my computer, was working. didn't pass it ( the car ) up yet. i'll take your advice on the sending unit.<P><BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Voytek:<BR><B>My '87 GT sticks to the upper left sometimes when I start the car.  It goes to normal position when I flick it and then works fine.<P>Could be two things:<BR>1.  The gauge itself sticks - a little bit of hassle (gauge disassembly) and some lubricant should get it working properly.<P>2.  The sending unit could be toast.  They're cheap (about $40 bucks here in Canada, I believe).  Very easy to replace too.  Just to the left on the engine block(looking from the back) below the ignition coil.  It's a brass fitting that has a wire or two (I don't remember) sticking out of it.<P>I wouldn't pass up the car just because of this.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good luck.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><p>[This message has been edited by AuNROD (edited 12-11-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000004||GTDude||12-11-2001||08:29 PM||||Find the coolant sender...not sure where it is....but it will have 1 blade type terminal.  Unplug the sender and turn the ign. switch on.  The gauge needle should go all the way to the right. Ground the sender wire and the needle should go all the way to the left.  If everything is working properly with the gauge this is how it will react and you simply need a new temp sender.  If it does not react like above, then it's in the wiring (not probable) or dash printed circuit (more probable) or the gauge itself (most probable)  Hope this helps.<P>GTDude<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
